        "tag": "title",
        "text": "84"
      },
      {
        "y": 23.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 286.1,
        "tag": "title",
        "text": "The way you say it"
      },
      {
        "y": 70.5,
        "x0": 75.1,
        "x1": 332.9,
        "tag": "title",
        "text": "A Verbs denoting volume: from quiet to loud"
      },
      {
        "y": 88,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 499.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Mumble and mutter are both usually negative: Stop mumbling! I can’t hear what you’re saying."
      },
      {
        "y": 100,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 436,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "He was muttering something under his breath – probably complaining, as usual."
      },
      {
        "y": 112,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 480.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Murmur can be more positive: They murmured their approval when he told them the plan."
      },
      {
        "y": 124,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 446.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "The phrase without a murmur means without any protest, complaint or comment:"
      },
      {
        "y": 136,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 491.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "They accepted it all without a murmur. I was surprised; normally they argue about everything."
      },
      {
        "y": 148,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 356.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Raise your voice can be used in positive or negative contexts:"
      },
      {
        "y": 160,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 427.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "You’ll have to raise your voice a bit. She’s a little hard of hearing. [speak louder]"
      },
      {
        "y": 172,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 389.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Don’t you raise your voice at me! [Do not speak in that loud, angry tone.]"
      },
      {
        "y": 184,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 428.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Shout, yell, scream, roar and shriek are all followed by at: Don’t shout at me!"
      },
      {
        "y": 196,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 512.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Yell often conveys urgency, anger, frustration; it is also used when there is much surrounding noise:"
      },
      {
        "y": 208,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 383.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "He yelled at the children to stop messing around with his computer."
      },
      {
        "y": 220,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 351.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "‘Stay where you are!’ he yelled above the noise of the traffic."
      },
      {
        "y": 232,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 477,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Shriek means loud and very high-pitched. It can be used in positive and negative contexts:"
      },
      {
        "y": 244,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 271.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "‘Oh, wow! That’s fantastic!’ she shrieked."
      },
      {
        "y": 256,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 506.3,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Roar suggests very loud volume, but deeper-pitched, like a lion. It is used in positive and negative"
      },
      {
        "y": 268.4,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 136.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "contexts:"
      },
      {
        "y": 280,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 363.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "The crowd roared as he kicked the ball into the back of the net."
      },
      {
        "y": 292,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 298.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "‘How dare you come in here!’ he roared angrily."
      },
      {
        "y": 313.3,
        "x0": 74.7,
        "x1": 378.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "B Verbs describing speech and styles of conversation"
      },
      {
        "y": 330.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 441.6,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Tom and Lily are always nagging (at) each other. [criticising faults or duties not done]"
      },
      {
        "y": 342.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 481.9,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Stop bickering over who sits by the window, you two! [arguing in an irritated way usually about"
      },
      {
        "y": 354.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 251.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "something petty; synonym = squabble]"
      },
      {
        "y": 366.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 487.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "They spent all evening slagging off their colleagues. [criticising in an insulting way; very informal]"
      },
      {
        "y": 378.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 472.4,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Jessie is always whining and whingeing. [complaining in an annoying way; informal, negative]"
      },
      {
        "y": 390.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 506.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "He’s been chatting away on the phone all morning. [suggests light, non-serious talk; note how away is"
      },
      {
        "y": 404.2,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 270.7,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "used to emphasise continuous/extended talk]"
      },
      {
        "y": 414.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 461.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "We always gossip about work when we go out together. [talk about people, rumours, etc.]"
      },
      {
        "y": 426.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 514.5,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "It took him a long time to realise they were winding him up. [/ˈwaɪndɪŋ/ teasing, fooling him; informal;"
      },
      {
        "y": 438.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 337.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "noun = wind-up, often used in phrase, a complete wind-up]"
      },
      {
        "y": 450.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 497.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "I realised she was buttering me up. [saying nice things because she wanted something from me; negative]"
      },
      {
        "y": 462.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 508.2,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Rick tends to exaggerate his problems, so don’t take him too literally. [make something seem greater"
      },
      {
        "y": 474.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 247.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "than it really is; noun = exaggeration]"
      },
      {
        "y": 486.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 500.1,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "Meg is inclined to generalise on the basis of her very limited experience. [make general statements"
      },
      {
        "y": 498.9,
        "x0": 99.2,
        "x1": 261.8,
        "tag": "main",
        "text": "about something; noun = generalisation]"
      },
      {
        "y": 520.2,
        "x0": 75,
